  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern will continue into the\nweekend as an upper trough digs into the eastern CONUS and a\nweakening cold front approaches from the northwest. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms across the region this\nafternoon with plenty of moisture in instability in place and a\nremnant front dissipating across the Carolinas. Storms have\nremained relatively weak so far with with MLCAPE around\n1500-2000 J/Kg despite shear increasing to around 25-30 kt, so\nthere remains the potential for a few strongers storms\ndeveloping this afternoon and early evening. KMHX 12z sounding\nshows Pwat of 2.15\" and could see locally heavy rainfall,\nespecially if stronger storms develop. Cyclonic flow with the\npotential for weak embedded shortwave moving across the region\nwill keep the chance for showers and a few thunderstorms\novernight. \n\nThe threat for severe storms increases on Friday as the upper\ntrough axis pushes across the area bringing increasing jet\ndynamics with the attendant weakening cold front pushing into\nthe region. Instability is progged to be a bit higher tomorrow \nwith MLCAPES forecast to peak around 2000-2500 J/Kg with similar\nshear in place. Pwat values will continue to be north of 2\" \nbringing the threat for wet microbursts producing damaging wind \ngusts and SPC has parts in the Mid-Atlantic outlooked in \nmarginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe storms. Greater upper \nlevel support and covergence along the sfc front and other\nlingering boundaries may also lead to locally heavy rainfall\nbringing an increased risk for flash flooding and WPC has a\nslight risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall across the\nregion. \n\nContinued unsettled on Saturday but the upper trough begins to\nlift north of the area with height rises aloft and weakening  \ndynamics throughout the day bringing a decreasing threat for\nsevere thunderstorms but there remains a marginal risk (1 of 4)\nfor excessive rainfall. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Upper ridging builds into the region from the\nwest for the early to mid portion of next week bringing lower\nchances for showers and thunderstorms, though near climo chances\nfor storms along the sea breeze will persist through peak\nheating each day. The main story will be the building heat with\nhighs expected to climb into the 90s early next week and peaking\nin the mid 90s as we move into the middle of the week with heat\nheadlines possible.",
